DATE(1)                        P32W Unix                         DATE(1)

NAME
       date - print or set the date and time

SYNOPSIS
       date [-u] [-r seconds] [+format]
       date [-u] [cc]yymmddhhmm[.ss]
       date [-u] -s seconds

DESCRIPTION
       With no argument date prints the current date and time, in
       local time, in the form

              Wed Oct 14 12:30:45 PDT 2026

       The zone name is shown whenever a time zone is set (see
       below); in UTC it is left out:

              Wed Oct 14 19:30:45 2026

       An argument +format prints the time in that form instead:
       its characters are copied, except that each % field is
       replaced as listed under FORMAT.

       Given a time, root sets the clock and then prints the new
       time. The time is given as digits, as in Unix V7:

       cc     century (optional)
       yy     year; without cc, 2000 + yy
       mm     month, 01-12
       dd     day, 01-31 (up to the length of the month: 29
              February only in a leap year)
       hh     hour, 00-23
       mm     minute, 00-59
       .ss    second, 00-59 (optional; else 00)

       It is read as local time unless -u is given. The clock
       counts seconds since 1970 in 31 bits, so the year must be
       from 1970 to 2037.

       The hardware clock keeps UTC (Universal Time) and the
       battery-backed clock is set along with the system's, so a
       setting survives a reboot. Local time comes from the time
       zone: the TZ environment variable, or if it is unset or
       empty, the first line of /etc/TZ; with neither, UTC. Both
       hold a POSIX rule such as PST8PDT,M3.2.0,M11.1.0 (see tz(5)).

OPTIONS
       The options may come in any order.
       -u     print, or read the new setting, in UTC
       -r seconds
              print the time that many seconds after 00:00 UTC on
              1 January 1970, not the current time
       -s seconds
              set the clock to that many seconds since 00:00 UTC on
              1 January 1970 (digits only, at most 2147483647)

FORMAT
       %a %A  the day of the week: Wed, Wednesday
       %b %B  the month: Oct, October (%h is %b)
       %d %e  the day of the month: 01-31; 1-31 after a blank
       %m     the month, 01-12
       %y %Y  the year: 26, 2026; %C the century, 20
       %j     the day of the year, 001-366
       %H %I  the hour: 00-23, 01-12; %k and %l likewise, with a
              blank instead of the leading 0
       %M %S  the minute, the second: 00-59
       %p     AM or PM
       %w %u  the day of the week as a number: 0-6 from Sunday,
              1-7 from Monday
       %Z     the zone's name (UTC with -u or without a zone)
       %s     the seconds since 1970
       %D %x  %m/%d/%y         %F  %Y-%m-%d
       %T %X  %H:%M:%S         %R  %H:%M
       %r     %I:%M:%S %p      %c  %a %b %e %H:%M:%S %Y
       %n %t  a newline, a tab; %% a %
       Any other % and character is printed as it is.

EXAMPLES
       date                          the time here
       date -u                       the time in UTC
       date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M'        2026-10-14 12:30
       date -u -r 0                  Thu Jan  1 00:00:00 1970
       date 2610141230               set 12:30 on 14 Oct 2026
       date 202610141230.15          the same, with century and secs
       TZ=JST-9; date                Tokyo time (from now on, in
                                     this shell)

FILES
       /etc/TZ        the system's time zone

ENVIRONMENT
       TZ             the time zone, as a POSIX rule

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success; 1 if the clock could not be set (not root:
       "date: not owner"); 2 for a usage error, a malformed or
       impossible time ("date: 2602310000: bad date") or a bad
       number of seconds.

SEE ALSO
       uptime(1), time(2), time(3), tz(5)

BUGS
       There is no -d (a date in words). Two-digit years always
       mean 2000-2099. Times after 03:14:07 UTC on 19 January 2038
       cannot be represented.

       A zone whose standard name is UTC (such as TZ=UTC0) is not
       named in the default output.
